description: A table of contents no longer contains `tc-reveal` elements
impact-type: compatibility-break

The `toc` macros render expand and collapse state with the `<%if%>` conditional instead of `$reveal`. Every entry loses two wrappers around the chevron button, and every expandable entry loses one more around its nested list.

Before:

```
<div class="tc-toc-item-row">
	<span class="tc-reveal"><button class="tc-btn-invisible tc-popup-keep">...</button></span>
	<span class="tc-reveal" hidden="true"></span>
	<a class="tc-tiddlylink">...</a>
</div>
```

After:

```
<div class="tc-toc-item-row">
	<button class="tc-btn-invisible tc-popup-keep tc-toc-chevron">...</button>
	<a class="tc-tiddlylink tc-toc-link">...</a>
</div>
```

Custom CSS that reached the chevron button through `.tc-reveal` should use the new `tc-toc-chevron` class instead. Nested lists already carry `tc-toc`, and item links now carry `tc-toc-link`, so no rule needs to name a bare `button`, `a` or `ol`.

The practical risk is low. Every `$reveal` in a wiki emits `tc-reveal`, so the class identifies nothing in particular and makes a poor styling hook on its own. It was <<.deprecated-since 5.3.8>> for that reason, see [[Deprecated Core Classes]].
